=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none

diagnosis_text:
The NOC ticket is a false premise. R7's OSPF process has not died and R1 has
not lost routes to the R7 region.

Direct evidence from the live lab refutes both claims:

1. R1's routing table contains all three reported prefixes — 17.1.1.1/32
   through 17.1.4.1/32 (R7 loopbacks), 67.0.0.0/8 (R7 eth0), and
   70.0.0.0/8 (R7 eth1) — installed as OSPF routes via 31.1.1.1 on eth2,
   with the 17.1.1.1/32 entry last updated 3 minutes and 57 seconds ago.
   R1's own OSPF adjacencies (to neighbors 39.1.1.1 and 112.1.1.1) are
   both Full and have been stable for over 4 minutes.

2. R7's ospfd daemon (PID 60) has been running continuously since the lab
   started at 00:43:59; its FRR log shows a clean startup followed by a
   single adjacency negotiation with R6 (16.1.4.1) at 00:44:44 — no
   crashes, no restarts, no watchfrr-triggered respawns.

3. R7 holds a Full OSPF adjacency with R6 over the Y segment (67.1.1.1 ↔
   67.1.1.2). R6 independently confirms this adjacency as Full/DR state.
   No fault-injection kill scripts are present in R7's process table.

4. R7's FRR running configuration is correct: all six networks (four
   loopbacks in 17.1.0.0/8 and both physical subnets 67.0.0.0/8 and
   70.0.0.0/8) are properly advertised into area 0.0.0.1.

The fresh route ages seen at the start of the investigation (≈45 s) reflect
normal OSPF initial convergence after the lab was deployed roughly 3–4 minutes
before the check — not a fault. No corrective action is required.
